# INVERSE PROPERTY EXPRESSIONS ARE OUT-OF-PROFILE, NOT MALFORMED INPUT.
# `[ owl:inverseOf :p ]` is well-formed OWL 2 (ObjectInverseOf) and excluded by OWL 2 EL
# (ObjectPropertyExpression := ObjectProperty) — v1 territory. RO writes 10 of its property
# chains this way; treating the blank node as a decode error turned the whole document into
# exit 3 instead of an enumerated omission.
# Expect: one omission per axiom below, none for the declarations; verdict INCONCLUSIVE (exit 2).
@prefix : <http://example.org/t#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

:p a owl:ObjectProperty .
:q a owl:ObjectProperty .
:s a owl:ObjectProperty .
:A a owl:Class .
:B a owl:Class .

# A chain step (RO's shape).
:s owl:propertyChainAxiom ( [ owl:inverseOf :p ] :q ) .

# The object of a property inclusion (RO's other shape).
:q rdfs:subPropertyOf [ owl:inverseOf :p ] .

# The property of a restriction.
:A rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf :p ] ;
    owl:someValuesFrom :B ] .
